<main>

<h1>Guelph Trees</h1>
<p class="lead">
  A reference layer of the street and park trees in the City of Guelph's
  inventory &mdash; <strong>{{TREE_COUNT}}</strong> City- and boundary-owned
  trees with their genus, species and trunk diameter &mdash; ready to add to
  the <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org">OpenStreetMap</a> editors
  <strong>iD</strong> and <strong>JOSM</strong> while you map.
</p>
<p class="meta">City data: {{DATA_DATE}} &middot; tiles rebuilt: {{BUILD_DATE}}</p>

<div class="attribution">
  <a href="{{LICENSE_URL}}">{{LICENSE_NOTICE}}</a>
  Source: <a href="{{DATASET_PAGE}}">Trees</a>.
</div>

<div class="note">
  <strong>Before you copy anything:</strong> importing individual trees is
  contested in OpenStreetMap, and copying species and girth off this overlay
  tree by tree is, in effect, an attribute import. Use it as a reference: add
  a tree you can see on imagery, give it a species you have verified, and do
  not bulk-copy. The City's licence is compatible with OSM (the OSMF Licence
  Working Group approved it on 2024-09-09), but compatibility is not
  community consensus.
</div>

<h2>Preview</h2>
<div id="preview-map" data-raster-url="{{RASTER_URL}}"></div>
{{LEGEND}}
<p class="meta">
  The raster layer drawn over OpenStreetMap: a dot per tree, coloured by
  genus. From zoom 16 each crown is drawn to an approximate size estimated
  from the trunk diameter &mdash; a guide to &ldquo;big tree here&rdquo;, not a
  measured canopy.
</p>

<div class="attribution">
  <strong>Coverage vs OpenStreetMap:</strong> see
  <a href="gaps/">where OSM has no trees at all (by 250&nbsp;m cell), and
  which mapped trees lack the species and girth the City records</a> &mdash;
  a mapper's worklist with an interactive map.
</div>

<h2>Two layers &mdash; which to use</h2>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Vector layer</th>
    <td>Interactive. Click a tree to read its tags. Best in
        <strong>iD</strong>.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <th>Raster layer</th>
    <td>Trees drawn directly on the tiles, coloured by genus. A readable
        backdrop. Best in <strong>JOSM</strong>.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3>What the vector layer carries</h3>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th>OSM keys</th>
    <td><code>genus</code> (<em>Quercus</em>), <code>species</code>
        (<em>Quercus rubra</em>) and <code>circumference</code> &mdash; the
        trunk's girth in metres, which is OSM's key and unit, computed as
        DBH&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;&pi;&nbsp;/&nbsp;100.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <th>City-only fields</th>
    <td><code>common_name</code>, <code>dbh_cm</code>, <code>planted</code>,
        <code>owner</code>, <code>address</code>, <code>city_asset_id</code>.
        These are <strong>not</strong> OSM tags. <code>common_name</code> is
        often a cultivar (&ldquo;Crimson King Maple&rdquo;), not a species name.
        <code>city_asset_id</code> is the City's inventory number &mdash; it is
        not painted on the tree, so it is not a <code>ref</code>.</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2>Add to iD</h2>
<h3>Vector layer (interactive)</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Open the iD editor and press <kbd>U</kbd> for the <em>Map Data</em> panel.</li>
  <li>Under <em>Custom Map Data</em>, open the settings and paste this URL:</li>
</ol>
<pre class="url">{{VECTOR_URL}}</pre>
<p>Zoom in to 15 or closer, then click any tree to see its tags in the
   inspector.</p>
<figure>
  <a href="iD.jpg"><img src="iD.jpg" width="1500" height="900" loading="lazy"
     alt="iD with the trees layer as pink rings along Dufferin Street; a City Harlequin Maple is selected and its tags shown in the sidebar"></a>
  <figcaption>iD, Dufferin Street: the City's street trees are the pink rings,
    and clicking one shows its tags. The white tree pins are OSM's trees,
    mostly in back yards &mdash; the street trees here are not mapped yet.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>Raster layer (visible colours)</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Open the <em>Background</em> panel and choose <em>Custom</em>.</li>
  <li>Paste this URL template:</li>
</ol>
<pre class="url">{{RASTER_URL}}</pre>

<h2>Add to JOSM</h2>
<h3>Raster layer (recommended for JOSM)</h3>
<ol>
  <li>Open <em>Preferences &rarr; Imagery</em> and click <strong>+TMS</strong>.</li>
  <li>Paste this entry (the <code>tms[13,18]</code> prefix sets the zoom range;
      JOSM scales it up past z18):</li>
</ol>
<pre class="url">tms[13,18]:{{RASTER_URL_JOSM}}</pre>
<p>
  Give it a name such as <em>Guelph Trees</em>, click OK, then enable
  it from the <em>Imagery</em> menu. Note JOSM's placeholder is
  <code>{zoom}</code>, not <code>{z}</code>.
</p>
<figure>
  <a href="JOSM.jpg"><img src="JOSM.jpg" width="1500" height="938" loading="lazy"
     alt="JOSM with the trees raster over Esri imagery near Woolwich Street: City crowns coloured by genus along the streets, OSM's green tree icons in the yards"></a>
  <figcaption>JOSM, the same neighbourhood: the raster's crowns, coloured by
    genus, over Esri imagery and the OSM data layer.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>Vector layer (optional)</h3>
<p>
  JOSM can also add the vector tiles via <em>Preferences &rarr; Imagery</em> &rarr;
  <strong>+MVT</strong>:
</p>
<pre class="url">{{VECTOR_URL_JOSM}}</pre>

<h2>Notes</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Only trees the City owns, or that stand on a property line, are
      included. The City inventories some private trees too; they are left
      out. A removed tree drops out of the City's data rather than being
      flagged, so a tree on the imagery that this layer lacks may simply be
      private, and one this layer has may since have come down.</li>
  <li>The City's point is the trunk. A tree traced from imagery sits at the
      centre of the crown, which can be a few metres away.</li>
  <li>A trunk diameter over 300&nbsp;cm is a data-entry error, not a tree:
      such a tree keeps its genus and species but has no
      <code>circumference</code>, and is drawn at a default size.</li>
  <li>Vector tiles are pre-rendered for z14&ndash;19 (iD needs native tiles,
      so zoom in to 15 or closer); raster tiles for z13&ndash;18 (the editor
      overzooms them further).</li>
  <li>Tiles are refreshed weekly from the City's service.</li>
  <li>This is a <strong>reference</strong> layer &mdash; verify against survey
      or imagery before copying anything into OpenStreetMap.</li>
</ul>

